# Wing pod enclosure (v3)

When editing anything under `enclosures/pod/`:

1. **Read** [`enclosures/pod/REQUIREMENTS.md`](enclosures/pod/REQUIREMENTS.md) first (non-negotiables). Use [`pod_enclosure.md`](enclosures/pod/pod_enclosure.md) for layout/pneumatics detail and [`PRINT_RECIPE.md`](enclosures/pod/PRINT_RECIPE.md) for AnkerMake/PETG slice settings (supports are not in the STL).
2. **P0 is the governing constraint.** Every part must fit the AnkerMake M5C build volume 220 × 220 × 250 with ≥10 mm X/Y margin. Halves print flange-down and may be rotated 45°, so `OUTER_L + OUTER_H ≤ 297`. **Never buy bed fit by shortening the nose or tail** — that is exactly how v2 ended up with a 12 mm tail. Re-proportion the section, or split the part and say so.
3. **The outer mold line is ONE loft over ONE section family** (`section_wire`), and the inner cavity is the same call with `inset=WALL`. Do **not** rebuild it as a union of separate solids, and do **not** add a local pad or blister to the skin. v2's `mid ∪ nose ∪ tail ∪ panel-pad` is what produced both an 80 × 3.5 mm hole into the electronics bay and a body with a 12 mm tail. To change the shape, change `section_params` or the scale laws.
4. **Interior blanks** (flange rail, bulkheads, land, bay walls) must be `intersect`ed with `full_body_solid(0)` before union — they must not poke through the skin. Features that provably stay clear of the skin may be capped analytically with `skin_y_plus()` instead, for speed.
5. After geometry changes, regenerate and validate before calling anything print-ready:

```bash
cd enclosures/pod && uv run --project .. python wing_pod_v3.py
cd enclosures/pod && uv run --project .. python validate_pod.py
```

6. **Do not** claim STLs are AnkerMake-ready unless `validate_pod.py` exits 0. Watch **W1** (minimum wall) and **S1'** (full 3-D leak) in particular — those two exist because v2 passed every check it had while carrying an open slot into the electronics bay.
7. Preview PNGs: **unique filename per shape revision**. Never reuse a stale cached name.
8. Print contracts: flange-down, 45° baked into the STL export; **all printed-joint screws are M2.5 into heat-set inserts** (P6) with screw relief past the insert; **every board on raised standoffs** (I8 — fail empty `holes=[]`). Boards, static bay and static holes on `pod_right` only; aft-panel inserts are right-side only too (an X-axis boss would straddle the seam). Isolated static bay + `static_cover.stl` (S3). Pro Micro uses `pm_tray.stl` (L7). Service cluster is on the **aft face** on `tail_panel.stl` (L6) — nothing pierces the +Y skin except the static array. Pitot is **ESA SUN-B**, protruding 45 mm, clamped on its threaded band (L2). Mating face stays **open for install** (I4).
9. **Do not commit `.stl` / `.step` on every change.** They stay tracked, but they are ~12 MB of binary that churns on every regeneration — a STEP file rewrites its `FILE_NAME` timestamp and a few coordinates at the 1e-15 level even when the geometry is byte-identical. Commit code, docs and the QC PNGs freely. **Never add `.stl` or `.step` to a commit on your own judgement** — not even when the validator is green and the parts look right. A green validator is not the bar; the bar is that the user has *printed the parts, checked them, and said they are worth committing*. Every printed revision so far has been green and still had defects only the physical part revealed (a plate filling the cover, unreachable inserts, a slit in the nose, screw holes that did not penetrate). Ask, and wait. If you regenerate mid-work, `git checkout -- 'enclosures/pod/*.stl' 'enclosures/pod/*.step'` before committing.
10. Enclosure-only work continues on **`master`** (no PR) unless the user asks otherwise.
